==Finnish==
{{wikipedia|Apinat|lang=fi}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|fi|gmq-osw|apina}}, {{m|gmq-osw|apinia}}. Compare {{cog|sv|apinja}}, {{cog|gmq-bot|apunja||ape, monkey}}, from {{der|fi|non|apynja||female monkey}}. Compare {{cog|non|api}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{fi-p}}

===Noun===
{{fi-noun}}

# {{lb|fi|zoology}} [[simian]] {{gloss|ape, monkey or human; animal belonging to the infraorder Simiiformes within the order Primates}}
# [[monkey]] {{gloss|any nonhuman simian primate}}
#: {{ux|fi|Simpanssien tiedetään muodostavan laumoja ja metsästävän muita '''apinoita'''.|Chimpanzees are known to form bands to hunt and kill other '''monkeys'''.}}
# {{rfv-sense|fi}} [[ape]] {{gloss|primate of the clade Hominoidea, including e.g. gorillas, chimpanzees and gibbons}}
#: {{syn|fi|ihmisapina}}
#: {{ux|fi|Simpanssi muistaa Instagramin kuvien paikat paremmin kuin ihminen. Miten tämä on mahdollista? Ovathan ihmisen aivot paljon suuremmat kuin '''apinan'''.[https://yle.fi/aihe/artikkeli/2019/06/16/apinakin-osaa-kayttaa-instagramia-jopa-paremmin-kuin-ihminen]|A chimpanzee remembers the location of Instagram images better than a human. How is this possible? Man's brain is much larger than that of an '''ape''', isn't it?}} 
# {{lb|fi|informal|derogatory}} [[ape]] {{gloss|uncivilized person}}

====Usage notes====
* In zoology ''apina'' means any simian, including humans. In everyday language ''apina'' is understood to refer to any simian but NOT humans. In English "monkey" can be used similarly, but "ape" normally refers only to the most human-like simians of the clade Hominoidea, which the Finnish may call ''ihmisapina'' but usually don't, using mere ''apina'' instead. 

